#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120695} :activity/id #uuid "ad4d6410-a365-5f77-a4b1-3e2d134d5a3d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050638 :topic/id #uuid "5ae18ce0-90c2-55f7-8d81-bd5949f05aa3" :topic/math-academy-id 1864} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120695 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121042 :tutorial/content "Given a subspace $H$ with a basis $\\mathcal B = \\{ \\mathbf b_1, \\mathbf b_2 \\}$ and a vector $\\mathbf x \\in H,$ the **coordinates of the vector** $\\mathbf{x}$ **relative to the basis** $\\mathcal{B}$ are the coefficients ${\\color{red} x_1}$ and ${\\color{blue} x_2}$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}x_1} \\mathbf{b}_1+{\\color{blue}x_2} \\mathbf{b}_2 = \\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can write the vector $\\mathbf x$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal B$ using these coordinates, as follows:\n\n![](images/05/0593ca6a838b66c4623e8749eb5236d6259b1f9bf36ed0d68b83c49781076341.svg)\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\},$ where \n\n![](images/f3/f355faa0c477775f2a1cbb3522d0743cdbc9649a5ee9c55b579b1fac9ae2a661.svg)\n\nAlso let $H = \\textrm{span} \\{ \\mathbf b_1, \\mathbf b_2 \\},$ and consider the vector ![](images/78/78357fcf51c9cd6b7873f5bc35fcc214cb3790bcec00ee69d0932f15aacd39cb.svg)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf x$ can be expressed as a unique linear combination of $\\mathbf b_1$ and $\\mathbf b_2.$ That is to say, there exist *unique coefficients* $\\color{red}x_1$ and $\\color{blue}x_2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's solve for these coefficients. From the above, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of this system is \n\n![](images/82/82d4f494936d23e9b697aa4518edf68f7ea62e504d9d2576a6d13971acaf1c96.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = {\\color{red}3} \\mathbf{b}_1+ {\\color{blue}2} \\mathbf{b}_2,\n$$\n\nwhich can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/94/94b2934727a32284e9790e59ccd301bd858a0258843314a2968285a5eda7a302.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "bb4f07e6-7c2f-5fe2-b96b-1bb37603981a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3208 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "55354f89-d587-5329-9788-b198ebb218a3" :step/math-academy-id 17513 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120696}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120696 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119820 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119631 :question/id #uuid "0a0ea798-5125-5d0e-a755-96bf9af5f543" :question/math-academy-id "e-4823"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4f1675-9146-5279-9b64-07a63a9bed99" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048499}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409817}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Given Its Coordinates Relative To a Given Basis"} :step/id #uuid "b2734184-6a42-50f6-9a19-566236b06025" :step/math-academy-id 17514 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120757}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120697 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119821 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119632 :question/id #uuid "6c7237cd-c655-575f-83ac-7a0891c336b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4824"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "841f498b-f737-5086-8f79-44f1a143bebb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416946}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector Relative To a Given Basis Containing Two Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "94aa1ebf-d415-58aa-a0dc-17e8201c1669" :step/math-academy-id 17515 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120698}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120698 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119822 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119633 :question/id #uuid "90f07e38-405a-5f79-8501-0b4dfb8fa4c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4825"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed6a6ea8-6100-5224-b75f-b87c037fafbb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409821}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector Relative To a Given Basis Containing More Than Two Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "34b75a42-9505-5418-a27f-2b9aade2bc07" :step/math-academy-id 17516 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120756}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120756 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121066 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a set of vectors\n![](images/0d/0d78e1b2d01003885882f19b3856278e5024719f26f33594fd071fbd73273b71.svg)\n\nNotice that the set $\\mathcal{S}$ is linearly independent since the vector equation $x_1\\mathbf{e}_1+x_2\\mathbf{e}_2+x_3\\mathbf{e}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ leads to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich has only the zero solution. \n\nAlso, notice that any vector in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{e}_1,$ $\\mathbf{e}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{e}_3.$ For instance,\n![](images/ef/ef28dd8cbad6d256b9fa82c487263af3327ce630839cfcd1c7433417d0e0ae73.svg)\n\nIn particular, the equation above tells us that \n![](images/72/724f42afb534edbde7588c5508a4bf8de6d3cd068bc2989da7c7207b1bf7a1ba.svg)\nthat is to say, the numbers $\\color{red}-2,$ $\\color{red}0,$ $\\color{red}5$ are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{v}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{S}.$ \n\nThe basis $\\mathcal{S}$ is somewhat special, since the coordinates of a vector relative to this basis coincide with the components of the vector. We refer to $\\mathcal{S}$ as the **standard basis**. Remember that in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\mathbb{R}^3$ we even have special notations for the vectors of the standard basis:\n![](images/ae/aee5dd3226ebe950defc8b873399a884e0679ce701a3a55354ef01960e029f8e.svg)\n\n![](images/dd/dd96e0b70173adf8983feb4975c351ab3e417c34ed4a6a944bc391e33db4c8e9.png)\n\nIn general, the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n![](images/a8/a8e4b648898c0dc036e228fb84945833dc40c053beff034596e098c4dd077e23.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "54e7c1fd-c7bf-5c77-bcc5-eb8d93a11582" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3969 :tutorial/title "The Standard Basis"} :step/id #uuid "9053925c-8744-5312-b890-f5e4a1a8d2e8" :step/math-academy-id 23574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120757 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119859 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119674 :question/id #uuid "a79126f2-e26c-5929-93d8-825d85b9a438" :question/math-academy-id "e-6282"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2926764-dfbc-5e4e-a6b0-db24a7298a64" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409819}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector Relative To a Given Basis by Inspection"} :step/id #uuid "79095f38-ffd3-565b-a534-f136c7646422" :step/math-academy-id 23612 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120697}}] :activity/title "Expressing the Coordinates of a Vector in a Given Basis" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120054}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416947} :activity/id #uuid "e9bd4727-090c-5259-8cb9-53f91a718d53" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050638 :topic/id #uuid "5ae18ce0-90c2-55f7-8d81-bd5949f05aa3" :topic/math-academy-id 1864} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416947 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416946 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54b2da92-80e1-5a61-a19c-e0402c5db36e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416936} {:answer/id #uuid "94ec8a9d-591d-59ec-b9c5-a655500ebc04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416937} {:answer/id #uuid "c791cf81-feb5-59a1-bf25-b0be74c138f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\notin \\text{span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416940} {:answer/id #uuid "ccdbeb50-bd46-5e6a-a5d9-d2fa3f1bb293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416942} {:answer/id #uuid "f2716f4b-b756-5daf-b7ff-420d367fb480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416943}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416936} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bd25317-6ae7-5ed2-b2f8-06a74441129a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1098aac8-90b3-5ba0-b447-7ead2947d568" :question/math-academy-id "q-35247" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 32\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 32\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 9 & -8 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 16 & 32\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 9 & -8 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 16 & 32\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 20 \\\\ -12 & 16 & 32\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=-8 \\\\ 4{x}_{2}=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=5.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}-4(5)=-8\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4+5=9.$"} :step/id #uuid "52d64f85-27e5-5e6b-8c92-f165572fe095" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416948}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416948 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f5fc79-efcf-5beb-8f44-6c5bbee2e205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\notin \\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416934} {:answer/id #uuid "460c227b-da50-5240-a61b-5d7158cbefb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416935} {:answer/id #uuid "b96e7099-61f5-552d-a37f-75ea4d0acd7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416938} {:answer/id #uuid "c28d8b2c-0794-5b52-be1a-1a5068c24206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416939} {:answer/id #uuid "ca59db9a-208c-56e6-9d14-d390aecd3368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416941}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416935} :answer-field/id #uuid "c652bfa5-8e6a-53fc-ad6f-da352c6f194d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "93dd069a-4710-5057-b8fe-736d3ab4c0dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-75844" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,2\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,-4\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=2\\cdot {b}_{1}+(-4)\\cdot {b}_{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that ${x}_{2}=-4.$"} :step/id #uuid "598de137-99ae-5ba2-ac49-2dd82ea7ee2f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416949}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416949 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409821 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0470cd3b-2bcb-575e-9def-d43004406207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409787} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e208c3-ba1a-51ec-b367-c2784fabe06c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409800} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ba42a7-b00b-5980-9f89-48160c1eb49e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\notin \\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409801} {:answer/id #uuid "d13c159c-f442-5933-a821-d94880c3ba32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409806} {:answer/id #uuid "eec553dd-6fd7-5208-9292-850213e9c19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409809}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409801} :answer-field/id #uuid "a79bee7d-12b6-5017-ae13-ca00a861bd24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ec45397c-5ced-5a47-b9a9-86c6aa1d1bf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-76073" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$,  ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we get the system with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ \\,\\,-{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ \\,\\,0=5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the third equation is always false. Therefore, the system has no solution and we conclude that \n\n$$\nx\\notin \\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6074dbdf-0c2f-509e-aa43-e4c385bf7861"}] :activity/title "Expressing the Coordinates of a Vector in a Given Basis" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416933}]}
